# 8. Deploying

`dist/` is a directory of static files with no server requirements. Any host
will serve it. This chapter is about GitHub Pages, because that is the one
crabbucket is shaped around.

## Set the base path

A project site is served from `https://you.github.io/ferrite-docs/`. A user
site or a custom domain is served from the root.

```toml
# site.toml
base = "/ferrite-docs/"
url  = "https://you.github.io/ferrite-docs/"
```

`base` is what every link and asset is built from. `url` is optional and
turns on the things that need an absolute address: `sitemap.xml`,
`robots.txt`, feeds, and social cards. Without it those are skipped entirely
rather than emitted with guessed URLs.

This is the whole of what you have to get right, and [chapter
3](../routes-and-links/) is why it is only one line.

## The workflow

`crab new` writes it. Two jobs: build, then deploy.

```yaml
name: Pages

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

concurrency:
  group: pages
  cancel-in-progress: true
```

Then a build job that runs `crab build .` and uploads `dist/`, and a deploy
job that publishes it. Turn on Pages in the repository settings with "GitHub
Actions" as the source, once.

## What the build writes

```
dist/
  index.html
  docs/index.html      every route is a directory
  404.html             a file, because that is what hosts look for
  site.css
  router.js            only if the site asked for it
  search.json          only if site.toml asks for search
  search.js            "
  og/index.png         one card per page, if the design system draws them
  sitemap.xml          only with a url
  robots.txt           only with a url
  .nojekyll
```

## `.nojekyll`, which is the one that gets people

GitHub Pages runs uploaded output through Jekyll unless that file is present,
and Jekyll silently drops every file and directory whose name begins with an
underscore. Nothing errors. Some assets are just missing.

The build writes the file for you. It is a famously annoying thirty minutes
and you should not have to spend it.

## The same bytes every time

A build produces identical output on every machine. Line endings in what the
build writes are normalised, because a design system's CSS and clients arrive
through `include_str!` and a checkout on Windows carries carriage returns —
which would otherwise make the same site, built twice, two different sites.
Social cards are deterministic for the same reason.

That matters for a deploy that diffs its output, and it is the sort of thing
that is very cheap to do on day one and very annoying to retrofit.

## Somewhere that is not GitHub Pages

Set `base = "/"`, point the host at `dist/`, and there is nothing else. No
runtime, no server, no rewrite rules — every route is a directory containing
